@article{sano_learning_2020,
    author = "Sano, Megumi and Freitas, Julian De and Haber, Nick and Yamins, Daniel LK",
    abstract = "From an early age, humans are capable of learning about theirsocial environment, making predictions of how other agentswill operate and decisions about how they themselves will in-teract. In this work, we address the problem of formalizing thelearning principles underlying these abilities. We construct a cu-rious neural agent that can efficiently learn predictive models ofsocial environments that are rich with external agents inspiredby real-world animate behaviors such as peekaboo, chasing,and mimicry. Our curious neural agent consists of a controllerdriven by -Progress, a scalable and effective curiosity signal,and a disentangled world model that allocates separate networksfor interdependent components of the world. We show that ourdisentangled curiosity-driven agent achieves higher learning ef-ficiency and prediction performance than strong baselines. Cru-cially, we find that a preference for animate attention emergesnaturally in our model, and is a key driver of performance. Fi-nally we discuss future directions including applications of ourframework to modeling human behavior and designing earlyindicators for developmental variability.",
